{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>עֵץ פְּרִי עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי לְמִינוֹ.</b> For every tree is called a fruit tree, and all of them produce fruit — some for eating and some for healing. And what is said, 'עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי לְמִינוֹ,' may be explained as follows: that man is the tree of the field and resembles it, both in the form of his stature and in that he has eternal endurance like man, as it is said (Job 14:7), 'כִּי יֵשׁ לָעֵץ תִּקְוָה.' And all the grasses are related to the other living creatures, both in that they are low in stature and in that they have no root and branch, but are 'כְּצִיץ יָצָא וַיִּמָּל' (Job 14:2). And the Holy One, blessed be He, created every kind of food to be suited to the temperament of the one who eats, and joined every kind to its kind, as it is said, 'וּמַשְׂבִּיעַ לְכָל חַי רָצוֹן' — meaning, a thing that is his desire, in that it is close to his nature and temperament. Therefore it says, 'וּלְכָל חַיַּת הָאָרֶץ וְגוֹ׳ אֶת כָּל יֶרֶק עֵשֶׂב לְאָכְלָה,' for vegetation is suited only to the non-speaking material species of living creature. But to man He said, 'הִנֵּה נָתַתִּי לָכֶם אֶת כָּל עֵשֶׂב וְגוֹ׳ וְאֶת כָּל הָעֵץ וְגוֹ׳,' for from the aspect of the material part in man, vegetation too is suited to him, and from the aspect of the admixture of spirituality within him, the fruit of the tree is suited to him. And then each kind goes to its kind; therefore it says 'עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי לְמִינוֹ' — to the one who is its kind, that is, man. And 'עֵשֶׂב מַזְרִיעַ זֶרַע' did not mention 'לְמִינוֹ' in it, because it too is for man even though it is not his kind at all. But in the act of making he mentioned 'לְמִינֵהוּ,' from the aspect that it is, from one side, its kind — that is, for the material part in man." "And the Sages (Genesis Rabbah 5:9) said that the earth sinned in this, for the Holy One, blessed be He, said 'עֵץ פְּרִי' — that the taste of the tree should be like the taste of the fruit — and it did not do so. Therefore when man sinned, she too was punished for her iniquity and was cursed. And here one asks: why was she not cursed immediately? And it appears that this is not difficult at all, for the essence of the curse was that the earth would bring forth gnats and fleas, as Rashi explained on the verse (Genesis 3:17) 'אֲרוּרָה הָאֲדָמָה בַּעֲבוּרֶךָ,' and all these are things that harm man. And as long as man had not sinned, the earth was not cursed to bring forth accursed things, for even if the earth sinned, nevertheless — what is man's crime, that fleas and gnats should pursue him? But once man too sinned, both of them became worthy of this curse. And in the portion of Acharei Mot (Leviticus 18:25), on the verse 'וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ וָאֶפְקֹד עֲוֹנָהּ עָלֶיהָ,' it will be explained with the help of HaShem that the will of El, blessed be He, was that she give the creatures pure and refined matter, and she gave coarse and thick matter. And had she given pure and refined matter, every tree itself would have tasted like the fruit, and man too would not have been so drawn toward the materialities and would not have come to sin. However, since she transgressed and gave coarse matter, she caused it to become 'עֵץ עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי' and not 'עֵץ פְּרִי,' and this matter also caused man's sin, for through this cause man was drawn toward materiality and fell into sin. Therefore, for every sin of man, the earth is punished and her iniquity is remembered upon her, as will be explained with the help of HaShem." "And it appears proper to give another reason for the matter: for since the serpent saw that the earth had altered the will of her Creator and even so was not punished, therefore the serpent found an opening to say to the woman, 'לֹא מוֹת תְּמוּתוּן' — even if you transgress the will of your Creator — just as the earth was not punished for not having produced a tree whose taste is like the taste of the fruit. And therefore the serpent said, 'אַף כִּי אָמַר אֱלֹהִים לֹא תֹאכְלוּ מִכֹּל עֵץ הַגָּן' — meaning, is the tree even fit for eating, such that He needed to command you not to eat even of the tree? And the woman said, 'מִפְּרִי עֵץ הַגָּן נֹאכֵל' — I concede to your words that only the fruit of the tree of the garden is fit for eating, not the tree itself; therefore there was no need to command us except concerning the fruit of the tree that is within the garden, and not the tree. And the serpent said, 'לֹא מוֹת תְּמוּתוּן' — for behold, you concede that in this the earth altered the will of its Creator and even so was not punished; so too you will not die either.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:33:12.431813503Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}